# 3. Load Balancing ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

You already know AWS **ALB**, so now let's understand the **system-design concepts behind ALB**.

Load balancer:

> **Distributes incoming requests across multiple servers.**
> 

Without load balancing:

```
             Users
                │
                ▼
             Server
```

Problem:

```
Server crashes
     ↓
Application DOWN
```

With load balancing:

```
              Users
                │
                ▼
           Load Balancer
          /      |      \
         ↓       ↓       ↓
       Server  Server  Server
```

Benefits:

- scalability
- availability
- fault tolerance
- traffic distribution

---

# 3.1 L4 vs L7 ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Very common interview question.

## Layer 4 — Transport Layer

Works primarily with:

```
TCP
UDP
IP
Port
```

It doesn't need to understand the HTTP request itself.

Example:

```
Client
  ↓
L4 Load Balancer
  ↓
Server
```

AWS example:

```
Network Load Balancer (NLB)
```

Good for:

- very high throughput
- TCP
- UDP
- low latency
- non-HTTP protocols

---

# Layer 7 — Application Layer

Understands application-level protocols such as:

```
HTTP
HTTPS
```

It can inspect things like:

```
URL
HTTP method
Host
Headers
Cookies
```

Example:

```
GET /users
```

An L7 load balancer can route based on:

```
/api/*
/admin/*
/products/*
```

AWS example:

```
Application Load Balancer (ALB)
```

### Simple comparison

| L4 | L7 |
| --- | --- |
| TCP/UDP | HTTP/HTTPS |
| Lower-level | Application-level |
| Very fast | More intelligent routing |
| IP/port based | URL/header/host based |
| NLB | ALB |

### Interview answer

> **"L4 load balancing operates at the transport layer using IP, TCP or UDP information, while L7 operates at the application layer and can make routing decisions based on HTTP information such as paths, hosts and headers."**
> 

---

# 3.2 Reverse Proxy ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

This is another important concept.

Without reverse proxy:

```
User
  ↓
Node.js :3000
```

With reverse proxy:

```
User
  ↓
Nginx :443
  ↓
Node.js :3000
```

The user doesn't directly communicate with Node.js.

The reverse proxy receives the request and forwards it.

---

## Nginx Example

```
Browser
   ↓
https://example.com
   ↓
Nginx :443
   ↓
Node.js :3000
```

Nginx can handle:

- HTTPS
- static files
- routing
- compression
- reverse proxy
- connection handling

---

## ALB is also acting as a reverse-proxy-style intermediary

For example:

```
Internet
   ↓
ALB
   ↓
ECS/Fargate
   ↓
FastAPI :8000
```

The user doesn't directly access the FastAPI container.

---

# 3.3 Health Checks ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Load balancers need to know:

> **"Is this server healthy?"**
> 

Example:

```
GET /health
```

Response:

```
{
  "status": "ok"
}
```

Architecture:

```
                 ALB
              /       \
        Health OK    Health FAIL
           ↓             ↓
        Server A       Server B
                         ❌
```

If Server B fails its health checks, the load balancer stops sending new traffic to it.

---

## Example

```
ALB
 │
 ├── EC2-A → healthy ✅
 │
 ├── EC2-B → healthy ✅
 │
 └── EC2-C → unhealthy ❌
```

Traffic:

```
ALB
 ├── EC2-A
 └── EC2-B
```

Not:

```
ALB → EC2-C
```

---

# 3.4 Load-Balancing Algorithms ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

The load balancer needs a strategy to decide:

> **"Which server should receive this request?"**
> 

Common algorithms:

```
Round Robin
Least Connections
Weighted
IP Hash
Random
```

---

# 3.5 Round Robin ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Requests are distributed sequentially.

```
Request 1 → Server A
Request 2 → Server B
Request 3 → Server C
Request 4 → Server A
Request 5 → Server B
Request 6 → Server C
```

Diagram:

```
             Load Balancer
              /    |    \
             A     B     C
             ↑     ↑     ↑
             └─────┴─────┘
               repeat
```

Good when servers have roughly equal capacity and requests have similar cost.

---

# 3.6 Least Connections ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Send the new request to the server with the fewest active connections.

Suppose:

```
Server A → 20 connections
Server B → 5 connections
Server C → 12 connections
```

New request:

```
          ↓
       Server B
```

because B currently has the fewest connections.

Useful when requests have different durations.

---

# 3.7 Weighted Routing ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Not every server needs to receive equal traffic.

Suppose:

```
Server A = weight 70
Server B = weight 30
```

Approximately:

```
70% → A
30% → B
```

Useful for:

### Canary deployment

```
                  ALB
                /     \
              90%     10%
               ↓        ↓
           Old version New version
                       Canary
```

You can test the new version with a small percentage of traffic.

---

# 3.8 Sticky Sessions ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Normally:

```
Request 1 → Server A
Request 2 → Server B
Request 3 → Server C
```

Sticky sessions try to keep the same client associated with the same server.

```
User A
  ↓
ALB
  ↓
Server A

User A's next request
  ↓
ALB
  ↓
Server A
```

This is useful for some stateful applications.

But there's a disadvantage:

```
Server A becomes overloaded
```

while:

```
Server B → low traffic
Server C → low traffic
```

Also, if Server A fails, the session may be disrupted.

### Better modern approach

Make the application **stateless**.

Store shared session state externally:

```
             ECS
          /   |   \
        Task Task Task
          \    |   /
             Redis
```

Then any task can handle the request.

---

# 3.9 Stateless Services ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

This connects directly to load balancing.

Bad:

```
User
 ↓
Server A
 ↓
Session stored locally
```

Next request:

```
User
 ↓
Server B
 ↓
❌ Session not found
```

Better:

```
              ALB
            /  |  \
         ECS ECS ECS
            \  |  /
             Redis
```

Now:

```
Request → Task A → Redis
Request → Task B → Redis
Request → Task C → Redis
```

Any server can process the request.

This makes horizontal scaling much easier.

---

# 4. Caching ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Caching is one of the **most important system-design concepts**.

Basic idea:

> **Keep frequently accessed data in a faster storage layer so we don't repeatedly access the slower source.**
> 

Without cache:

```
User
 ↓
API
 ↓
Database
 ↓
Response
```

With cache:

```
User
 ↓
API
 ↓
Redis
 ↓
Cache HIT
 ↓
Response
```

The database is avoided.

---

# 4.1 Why Caching?

Caching provides:

### 1. Lower latency

```
Database → slower
Redis    → much faster
```

### 2. Lower database load

Without cache:

```
10,000 requests
       ↓
10,000 DB queries
```

With cache:

```
10,000 requests
       ↓
Redis
       ↓
9,000 cache hits
       ↓
1,000 DB queries
```

### 3. Higher throughput

Your application can handle significantly more read traffic when repeated reads are served from cache.

---

# 4.2 Cache Hit vs Cache Miss ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

### Cache Hit

Data exists:

```
Request
   ↓
Redis
   ↓
FOUND ✅
   ↓
Return data
```

### Cache Miss

Data isn't there:

```
Request
   ↓
Redis
   ↓
NOT FOUND ❌
   ↓
Database
   ↓
Store in Redis
   ↓
Return
```

---

# 4.3 Cache-Aside ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

This is probably the **most important caching pattern for interviews**.

Application manages the cache.

```
             Request
                ↓
              Redis
             /     \
          HIT       MISS
           ↓          ↓
        Return      Database
                       ↓
                    Redis
                       ↓
                    Return
```

### Example

Node.js:

```
const cached = await redis.get(`product:${id}`);

if (cached) {
  return JSON.parse(cached);
}

const product = await db.getProduct(id);

await redis.set(
  `product:${id}`,
  JSON.stringify(product),
  { EX: 300 }
);

return product;
```

Here:

```
EX: 300
```

means the cache expires after approximately **300 seconds**.

---

# 4.4 Read-Through Cache ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Application asks the cache:

```
Application
     ↓
   Cache
     ↓
  Database
```

The cache itself is responsible for loading missing data from the database.

Conceptually:

```
Application
     ↓
Cache
     ↓
if missing → DB
     ↓
Cache
```

Difference:

### Cache-aside

```
Application manages cache
```

### Read-through

```
Cache layer manages loading
```

---

# 4.5 Write-Through ⭐⭐⭐⭐

When writing:

```
Application
     ↓
Cache
     ↓
Database
```

The data is written to both cache and database as part of the cache's write-through behavior.

Conceptually:

```
WRITE
  ↓
Cache
  ↓
Database
```

Advantage:

```
Cache stays relatively fresh
```

Disadvantage:

```
Every write may involve cache + DB
```

---

# 4.6 Write-Back / Write-Behind ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Application writes to cache first:

```
Application
     ↓
Cache
     ↓
Later
     ↓
Database
```

Example:

```
WRITE
 ↓
Redis
 ↓
Return quickly
 ↓
Async processing
 ↓
Database
```

### Advantage

Very fast writes.

### Risk

If the cache fails before data reaches the database, data could potentially be lost depending on the implementation/durability guarantees.

Therefore this pattern needs careful design.

---

# 4.7 TTL — Time To Live ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

TTL tells the cache:

> **"How long should this value remain valid?"**
> 

Example:

```
product:123
TTL = 300 seconds
```

After 300 seconds:

```
Cache
  ↓
Expired ❌
```

Application gets the data from DB again.

### Why TTL?

Because cached data can become stale.

Example:

```
Product price = ₹1,000
```

Cache:

```
₹1,000
```

Database changes:

```
₹900
```

Without expiration/invalidation:

```
Cache → ₹1,000 ❌
```

---

# 4.8 Cache Invalidation ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

One famous engineering problem:

> **"There are only two hard things in Computer Science: cache invalidation and naming things."**
> 

Suppose:

```
DB:
Product = ₹1000

Redis:
Product = ₹1000
```

User changes price:

```
DB → ₹900
```

But Redis still contains:

```
₹1000
```

We can invalidate:

```
DB update
   ↓
Redis DEL product:123
```

Next request:

```
Redis → MISS
   ↓
DB → ₹900
   ↓
Redis → ₹900
```

---

# 4.9 Cache Eviction ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

What happens when the cache becomes full?

The cache needs to remove something.

This is **eviction**.

Common policies:

```
LRU
LFU
FIFO
Random
TTL-based
```

---

# 4.10 LRU — Least Recently Used ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

LRU removes the item that hasn't been accessed recently.

Suppose cache can store 3 items:

```
A B C
```

Access:

```
A
```

Then:

```
B
```

Then:

```
D
```

Cache needs space.

If C was least recently used:

```
C ❌ removed

A B D
```

So:

> **LRU = remove the least recently accessed item.**
> 

This is extremely common in interviews.

---

# 4.11 Cache Stampede ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Also called:

> **Thundering herd problem**
> 

Suppose:

```
Popular product
```

is cached.

Then suddenly:

```
TTL expires
```

10,000 users request it simultaneously.

```
10,000 requests
       ↓
     Redis
       ↓
   MISS MISS MISS...
       ↓
   Database
       ↓
10,000 DB queries 😱
```

Database gets overloaded.

### Solutions

**1. Locking**

Allow one request to refresh the cache.

```
Request A → DB
Request B → wait
Request C → wait
Request D → wait
```

Then all use the refreshed cache.

**2. Early refresh**

Refresh before expiration.

**3. Randomized TTL**

Instead of all keys expiring at exactly the same time.

---

# 4.12 Cache Penetration ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

This occurs when requests are repeatedly made for data that **doesn't exist**.

Example:

```
GET /product/999999999
```

Database:

```
NOT FOUND
```

Cache:

```
NOT FOUND
```

If an attacker sends:

```
1,000,000 random IDs
```

you may get:

```
Requests
   ↓
Cache MISS
   ↓
Database
   ↓
NOT FOUND
```

Database gets hammered.

### Solution: Cache negative results

For example:

```
product:999999999 = NOT_FOUND
TTL = 60 sec
```

Then repeated requests don't hit the database.

Other defenses:

- validation
- rate limiting
- Bloom filters

---

# 4.13 Cache Breakdown ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Sometimes a very popular key expires or disappears while many users request it.

Conceptually:

```
Popular key
    ↓
Expires
    ↓
Many requests
    ↓
Database overload
```

This overlaps strongly with the **cache stampede/thundering herd** problem.

Solutions:

```
Distributed locks
Hot-key protection
Early refresh
Longer TTL
Request coalescing
```

---

# 4.14 Redis ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Redis is an in-memory data store commonly used for:

```
Caching
Sessions
Rate limiting
Counters
Distributed locks
Queues/streams
Temporary data
```

Example:

```
              API
               │
               ▼
             Redis
            /     \
         HIT       MISS
          │          │
          │          ▼
          │          RDS
          │          │
          │          ▼
          └────── Redis
```

### AWS equivalent

```
Amazon ElastiCache for Redis
```

or newer AWS managed Redis-compatible offerings depending on the architecture/service choice.

---

# 4.15 CDN Caching ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

A CDN caches content close to users.

Example:

```
India User
    ↓
CloudFront Edge
    ↓
Cache HIT
```

Instead of:

```
India User
    ↓
Internet
    ↓
AWS Region
    ↓
Application
```

This reduces latency and origin load.

Excellent for:

```
Images
CSS
JavaScript
Videos
Fonts
Static Next.js assets
Public content
```

Architecture:

```
                 Users
                   │
                   ▼
               CloudFront
              /    |     \
             ↓     ↓      ↓
           Edge  Edge    Edge
             \     |      /
                   ↓
                Origin
             S3 / ALB
```

---

# 4.16 Browser Caching ⭐⭐⭐⭐

The browser itself can cache resources.

For example:

```
GET /app.js
```

Response headers:

```
Cache-Control: max-age=3600
```

The browser can reuse the file for the specified period instead of downloading it again.

This is extremely useful for:

```
CSS
JS
Images
Fonts
Static assets
```

---

# 🔥 Complete Caching Architecture

For your React/Next.js + FastAPI/Node.js type application:

```
                    User
                      │
                      ▼
                 Browser Cache
                      │
                      ▼
                 CloudFront
                      │
                Cache HIT?
                /        \
              YES        NO
               │          │
               │          ▼
               │          ALB
               │          │
               │          ▼
               │       ECS/Fargate
               │          │
               │          ▼
               │        Redis
               │       /     \
               │     HIT      MISS
               │      │         │
               │      │         ▼
               │      │        RDS
               │      │         │
               │      └──── Redis
               │
               ▼
             Response
```

There are potentially **three caching layers**:

```
Browser Cache
      ↓
CDN Cache
      ↓
Application Cache / Redis
      ↓
Database
```

Each layer has a different purpose.

---

# 🔥 Load Balancing + Caching Together

Now combine both topics:

```
                         Users
                           │
                           ▼
                       CloudFront
                           │
                      Cache MISS
                           │
                           ▼
                          ALB
                    ┌──────┼──────┐
                    ▼      ▼      ▼
                  ECS    ECS     ECS
                    \      |      /
                     \     |     /
                       Redis
                         │
                       MISS
                         │
                         ▼
