# 🧠 10 Answers You Should Memorize

**CAP:**

> "During a network partition, a distributed system cannot simultaneously guarantee both strong consistency and availability for the affected operations."
> 

**Partial failure:**

> "A partial failure occurs when one component of a distributed system fails while other components continue operating."
> 

**Eventual consistency:**

> "Replicas may temporarily contain different values, but they eventually converge to the same state."
> 

**Idempotency:**

> "An idempotent operation can be safely repeated without causing additional unintended side effects."
> 

**Retry:**

> "Retries should use bounded exponential backoff with jitter to avoid overwhelming an already failing service."
> 

**Distributed lock:**

> "A distributed lock coordinates multiple instances so that only one or a controlled number of instances perform a particular operation."
> 

**Rate limiting:**

> "Rate limiting controls how many requests a client can make over time, protecting the system from abuse, overload, and unfair resource consumption."
> 

**Token bucket:**

> "Token bucket allows requests to consume tokens that are replenished at a fixed rate, allowing controlled bursts while enforcing an average rate."
> 

**Event-driven architecture:**

> "Services communicate by publishing and consuming events through a broker, which decouples producers from consumers and enables asynchronous processing."
> 

**DLQ:**

> "A dead-letter queue stores messages that repeatedly fail processing so they can be investigated or handled separately instead of retrying indefinitely."
> 
