> # 12. Reliability & Fault Tolerance ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> This section answers a very important system-design question:
> 
> > **"What happens when something goes wrong?"**
> > 
> 
> A good system doesn't assume that servers, networks, databases, or external APIs will always work.
> 
> Instead:
> 
> ```
> Failure
>    ↓
> Detect
>    ↓
> Contain
>    ↓
> Retry / Failover / Recover
>    ↓
> Continue serving users
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # 1. Timeout ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> Never allow a network request to wait forever.
> 
> Without timeout:
> 
> ```
> API A
>   │
>   │ request
>   ▼
> API B ❌
>   │
>   │ no response
>   ▼
> API A waits forever
> ```
> 
> With timeout:
> 
> ```
> API A
>   │
>   ▼
> API B
>   │
>   X
>   │ 5 seconds
>   ▼
> TIMEOUT
> ```
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> const controller = new AbortController();
> 
> setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000);
> 
> await fetch(url, {
>   signal: controller.signal
> });
> ```
> 
> ### Why timeout?
> 
> Without timeouts:
> 
> ```
> Slow dependency
>       ↓
> Connections remain occupied
>       ↓
> More requests wait
>       ↓
> Thread/connection pool exhausted
>       ↓
> Your service becomes unavailable
> ```
> 
> ### Interview answer
> 
> > "Timeouts prevent a service from waiting indefinitely for a slow or unavailable dependency and help prevent resource exhaustion."
> > 
> 
> ---
> 
> # 2. Retry ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> If a request fails because of a temporary problem, retry it.
> 
> ```
> API
>  ↓
> Payment Service
>  ↓
> Timeout ❌
>  ↓
> Retry
>  ↓
> Success ✅
> ```
> 
> But **don't retry everything**.
> 
> Good retry candidates:
> 
> ```
> Temporary network error
> Timeout
> 503 Service Unavailable
> Transient infrastructure failure
> ```
> 
> Be careful with:
> 
> ```
> 400 Bad Request
> 401 Unauthorized
> 403 Forbidden
> Validation errors
> ```
> 
> Retrying these usually won't help.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 3. Exponential Backoff ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> Don't retry immediately over and over.
> 
> Bad:
> 
> ```
> Failure
>  ↓
> Retry immediately
>  ↓
> Failure
>  ↓
> Retry immediately
>  ↓
> Failure
> ```
> 
> Better:
> 
> ```
> Attempt 1 → immediately
> Attempt 2 → 100ms
> Attempt 3 → 200ms
> Attempt 4 → 400ms
> Attempt 5 → 800ms
> ```
> 
> Conceptually:
> 
> ```
> delay = base × 2^attempt
> ```
> 
> Usually put a maximum limit:
> 
> ```
> 100ms
> 200ms
> 400ms
> 800ms
> 1600ms
> ...
> MAX = 5 seconds
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # 4. Jitter ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> Exponential backoff alone can still create a problem.
> 
> Imagine 10,000 servers fail simultaneously:
> 
> ```
> 10,000 requests
>       ↓
> all retry after 1 sec
>       ↓
> 10,000 requests
>       ↓
> service overloaded again
> ```
> 
> This is called a **retry storm**.
> 
> Add random jitter:
> 
> ```
> Server A → retry after 1.1 sec
> Server B → retry after 1.4 sec
> Server C → retry after 0.9 sec
> Server D → retry after 1.7 sec
> ```
> 
> Traffic gets spread out.
> 
> ### Interview answer
> 
> > "Jitter adds randomness to retry delays so many clients don't retry simultaneously and create a retry storm."
> > 
> 
> ---
> 
> # 5. Circuit Breaker ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> One of the most important reliability patterns.
> 
> Suppose:
> 
> ```
> Order Service
>      ↓
> Payment Service ❌
> ```
> 
> Payment is completely down.
> 
> Without circuit breaker:
> 
> ```
> Request
>  ↓
> Payment ❌
>  ↓
> Retry
>  ↓
> Payment ❌
>  ↓
> Retry
>  ↓
> Payment ❌
> ```
> 
> Your Order Service wastes resources repeatedly calling a dead dependency.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Circuit breaker
> 
> It behaves like an electrical circuit breaker.
> 
> ### CLOSED
> 
> Normal operation:
> 
> ```
> Order
>  ↓
> Payment
>  ↓
> Success
> ```
> 
> ### OPEN
> 
> Too many failures:
> 
> ```
> Order
>  ↓
> Circuit Breaker
>  ↓
> OPEN
>  ↓
> Don't call Payment
> ```
> 
> The request can immediately fail or use a fallback.
> 
> ### HALF-OPEN
> 
> After some recovery time:
> 
> ```
> Circuit OPEN
>     ↓
> wait
>     ↓
> HALF-OPEN
>     ↓
> test request
> ```
> 
> If successful:
> 
> ```
> HALF-OPEN
>     ↓
> CLOSED
> ```
> 
> If failed:
> 
> ```
> HALF-OPEN
>     ↓
> OPEN
> ```
> 
> ### Complete state machine
> 
> ```
>              failures
>  CLOSED ─────────────────→ OPEN
>     ↑                        │
>     │                        │ timeout
>     │                        ▼
>     └──────── success ── HALF-OPEN
>                             │
>                          failure
>                             │
>                             ▼
>                            OPEN
> ```
> 
> ### Important distinction
> 
> **Retry** says:
> 
> > "Try the dependency again."
> > 
> 
> **Circuit breaker** says:
> 
> > "Stop calling the failing dependency for a while."
> > 
> 
> They often work together.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 6. Bulkhead Pattern ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> The name comes from ships.
> 
> A ship has separate compartments:
> 
> ```
> ┌────────┬────────┬────────┐
> │ Engine │ Cargo  │ Engine │
> │        │        │        │
> └────────┴────────┴────────┘
> ```
> 
> If one compartment floods, the entire ship doesn't necessarily sink.
> 
> Same idea in software.
> 
> Suppose:
> 
> ```
> API
> ├── Payment
> ├── Email
> ├── Search
> └── Reports
> ```
> 
> If Reports consumes all worker threads:
> 
> ```
> Reports → 100% resources ❌
> Payment → cannot execute ❌
> ```
> 
> With bulkheads:
> 
> ```
> API
> ├── Payment → 20 connections
> ├── Email   → 10 connections
> ├── Search  → 30 connections
> └── Reports → 10 connections
> ```
> 
> Reports cannot consume everything.
> 
> ### Interview answer
> 
> > "The bulkhead pattern isolates resources between workloads so failure or overload in one component doesn't exhaust resources for the entire system."
> > 
> 
> ---
> 
> # 7. Health Check ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> Health checks determine whether a service is healthy enough to receive traffic.
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> GET /health
> ```
> 
> Response:
> 
> ```
> {
>   "status": "ok"
> }
> ```
> 
> Architecture:
> 
> ```
>               ALB
>            /   |   \
>           ▼    ▼    ▼
>         API1 API2 API3
>          ✅   ❌   ✅
> ```
> 
> ALB stops sending traffic to API2.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Liveness vs Readiness
> 
> Important distinction in modern systems.
> 
> ### Liveness
> 
> > "Is the application process alive?"
> > 
> 
> ```
> /health/live
> ```
> 
> If false, the platform may restart the application.
> 
> ### Readiness
> 
> > "Is the application ready to receive traffic?"
> > 
> 
> ```
> /health/ready
> ```
> 
> For example:
> 
> ```
> Application process → running
> Database connection → unavailable
> ```
> 
> The application may be alive but **not ready**.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 8. Failover ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> Failover means switching from a failed component to a backup.
> 
> Example database:
> 
> ```
>              Primary
>                │
>                X
>              FAILED
>                │
>                ▼
>              Standby
>                ✅
> ```
> 
> Traffic changes:
> 
> ```
> Before:
> 
> Application → Primary
> 
> After:
> 
> Application → Standby
> ```
> 
> Examples:
> 
> - database Multi-AZ
> - redundant application servers
> - multiple availability zones
> - multiple load-balancer targets
> 
> ---
> 
> # 9. Graceful Degradation ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> This means:
> 
> > **When part of the system fails, provide a reduced but useful experience instead of completely failing.**
> > 
> 
> Example e-commerce:
> 
> ```
> Normal:
> 
> Product
>  + Reviews
>  + Recommendations
>  + Wishlist
>  + Cart
>  + Payment
> ```
> 
> Recommendation service fails:
> 
> ```
> Product
>  + Reviews
>  + Wishlist
>  + Cart
>  + Payment
>  - Recommendations
> ```
> 
> The user can still buy the product.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Example
> 
> ```
> Recommendation API ❌
>        ↓
> Show popular products
>        ↓
> Continue application
> ```
> 
> Instead of:
> 
> ```
> Recommendation API ❌
>        ↓
> Entire page ❌
> ```
> 
> ### Interview answer
> 
> > "Graceful degradation allows the system to provide reduced functionality when a non-critical dependency fails."
> > 
> 
> ---
> 
> # 10. Load Shedding ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> Sometimes the system is overloaded.
> 
> Suppose:
> 
> ```
> System capacity = 10,000 RPS
> 
> Traffic = 50,000 RPS
> ```
> 
> Trying to process everything may cause:
> 
> ```
> CPU 100%
> Memory exhausted
> Connection pool exhausted
> Timeouts
> Cascading failure
> ```
> 
> Instead, deliberately reject some traffic.
> 
> ```
> 50,000 RPS
>      ↓
> Load Shedding
>      ↓
> 10,000 processed
> 40,000 rejected/deferred
> ```
> 
> Responses might include:
> 
> ```
> 429 Too Many Requests
> ```
> 
> or:
> 
> ```
> 503 Service Unavailable
> ```
> 
> depending on the situation and API semantics.
> 
> ### Goal
> 
> > **Keep the system alive instead of allowing overload to destroy the entire service.**
> > 
> 
> ---
> 
> # 11. Dead-Letter Queue ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> We covered this in Event-Driven Architecture, but it belongs here too.
> 
> Suppose:
> 
> ```
> OrderCreated
>      ↓
> Payment Worker
>      ↓
> FAIL
> ```
> 
> Retry:
> 
> ```
> Retry 1 ❌
> Retry 2 ❌
> Retry 3 ❌
> ```
> 
> Then:
> 
> ```
>              Queue
>                │
>            Processing
>                │
>           ┌────┴────┐
>           │         │
>        Success     Failure
>           │         │
>           ▼         ▼
>        Complete     DLQ
> ```
> 
> DLQ prevents:
> 
> ```
> Infinite retry
> ```
> 
> and allows:
> 
> ```
> Investigation
> Manual recovery
> Reprocessing
> Monitoring
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # 🔥 How These Patterns Work Together
> 
> This is the important part for system design.
> 
> Imagine:
> 
> ```
>                  Order Service
>                        │
>                        ▼
>                 Circuit Breaker
>                        │
>                        ▼
>                  Payment API
> ```
> 
> Payment becomes slow:
> 
> ```
> Payment
>    ↓
> Timeout
>    ↓
> Retry
>    ↓
> Exponential Backoff
>    ↓
> Jitter
> ```
> 
> Payment continues failing:
> 
> ```
> Failures
>    ↓
> Circuit Breaker OPEN
>    ↓
> Stop calling Payment
> ```
> 
> Meanwhile:
> 
> ```
> Order Service
>    ↓
> Graceful degradation
>    ↓
> Order = PAYMENT_PENDING
> ```
> 
> If payment events are asynchronous:
> 
> ```
> Payment Queue
>      ↓
> Worker
>      ↓
> Retry
>      ↓
> Retry
>      ↓
> DLQ
> ```
> 
> And if the entire service becomes overloaded:
> 
> ```
> Traffic
>   ↓
> Rate Limiter
>   ↓
> Load Shedding
>   ↓
> Protect system
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # 🔥 Complete Reliability Architecture
> 
> ```
>                          Users
>                            │
>                            ▼
>                      Rate Limiter
>                            │
>                            ▼
>                           ALB
>                            │
>                   ┌────────┼────────┐
>                   ▼        ▼        ▼
>                  API1     API2     API3
>                   │        │        │
>                   └────────┼────────┘
>                            │
>                     Circuit Breaker
>                            │
>                   ┌────────┼────────┐
>                   ▼        ▼        ▼
>                 Redis      RDS     Payment
>                                       │
>                                   Timeout
>                                       │
>                                     Retry
>                                       │
>                               Exponential Backoff
>                                       │
>                                     Jitter
>                                       │
>                               Circuit Breaker
>                                       │
>                                  if persistent
>                                       ▼
>                                   Fallback
> ```
> 
> Async side:
> 
> ```
> Order Service
>       │
>       ▼
>  Event / Queue
>       │
>       ▼
>    Worker
>       │
>  ┌────┴─────┐
>  │          │
> Success    Failure
>  │          │
>  ▼          ▼
> Done       Retry
>             │
>        Max retries
>             │
>             ▼
>            DLQ
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # ⭐ Reliability Patterns — Easy Comparison
> 
> | Pattern | Main Problem Solved |
> | --- | --- |
> | **Timeout** | Dependency takes too long |
> | **Retry** | Temporary failure |
> | **Exponential backoff** | Prevent rapid repeated retries |
> | **Jitter** | Prevent synchronized retry storms |
> | **Circuit breaker** | Stop calling a failing dependency |
> | **Bulkhead** | Prevent one workload exhausting all resources |
> | **Health check** | Detect unhealthy instances |
> | **Failover** | Switch to backup |
> | **Graceful degradation** | Continue with reduced functionality |
> | **Load shedding** | Protect system during overload |
> | **DLQ** | Isolate permanently failing messages |
> 
