> 
> # 13. Authentication & Security ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> Now we move from **availability** to **who can access what**.
> 
> The most important distinction:
> 
> ```
> Authentication
>       ↓
> WHO are you?
> 
> Authorization
>       ↓
> WHAT are you allowed to do?
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # 1. Authentication ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> Authentication verifies identity.
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> Email
> Password
>    ↓
> Login
>    ↓
> Identity verified
>    ↓
> User authenticated
> ```
> 
> Common mechanisms:
> 
> ```
> Password
> OTP
> JWT
> OAuth
> SSO
> Biometrics
> Passkeys
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # 2. Authorization ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> After authentication:
> 
> > What is this user allowed to access?
> > 
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> Aditya
> Role = ADMIN
> ```
> 
> Can:
> 
> ```
> Create user      ✅
> Delete user      ✅
> View reports     ✅
> ```
> 
> Normal user:
> 
> ```
> Role = USER
> 
> Create user      ❌
> Delete user      ❌
> View own data    ✅
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # Authentication vs Authorization
> 
> | Authentication | Authorization |
> | --- | --- |
> | Who are you? | What can you do? |
> | Login | Permissions |
> | Identity | Access control |
> | Happens first | Happens after identity |
> 
> ### Easy memory
> 
> > **AuthN = Authentication**
> > 
> 
> > **AuthZ = Authorization**
> > 
> 
> ---
> 
> # 3. JWT ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> JWT = JSON Web Token.
> 
> Typical structure:
> 
> ```
> HEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE
> ```
> 
> Conceptually:
> 
> ```
> User Login
>     ↓
> Server verifies credentials
>     ↓
> JWT generated
>     ↓
> Client
> ```
> 
> Then:
> 
> ```
> Authorization: Bearer <JWT>
> ```
> 
> Request:
> 
> ```
> Client
>   ↓
> JWT
>   ↓
> API
>   ↓
> Verify token
>   ↓
> Allow / deny
> ```
> 
> JWT commonly contains claims such as:
> 
> ```
> {
>   "sub": "user123",
>   "role": "admin",
>   "exp": 1790000000
> }
> ```
> 
> ### Important
> 
> JWT is **signed**, not automatically encrypted.
> 
> Don't put sensitive secrets into the payload just because it's encoded.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 4. OAuth 2.0 ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> OAuth 2.0 is primarily an **authorization framework**.
> 
> It allows an application to obtain limited access to a resource on behalf of a user.
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> Your App
>    ↓
> "Access user's Google data"
>    ↓
> Google authorization
>    ↓
> Access token
>    ↓
> Your App
> ```
> 
> OAuth answers:
> 
> > "What access has been delegated?"
> > 
> 
> ---
> 
> # 5. OpenID Connect ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> OIDC = OpenID Connect.
> 
> It builds an **identity/authentication layer on top of OAuth 2.0**.
> 
> OAuth:
> 
> ```
> Authorization
> ```
> 
> OIDC:
> 
> ```
> Authentication + identity
> ```
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> Login with Google
>         ↓
> Google
>         ↓
> OIDC
>         ↓
> Identity information
>         ↓
> Your application
> ```
> 
> ### Interview answer
> 
> > "OAuth 2.0 is primarily for delegated authorization, while OpenID Connect adds an authentication and identity layer on top of OAuth 2.0."
> > 
> 
> ---
> 
> # 6. SSO ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> SSO = Single Sign-On.
> 
> User logs in once:
> 
> ```
>              Identity Provider
>                     │
>              ┌──────┼──────┐
>              ▼      ▼      ▼
>            App A  App B  App C
> ```
> 
> The user doesn't need to separately log into every application.
> 
> Common enterprise technologies include:
> 
> ```
> OIDC
> SAML
> OAuth 2.0
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # 7. Access Token ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> An access token is used to access protected resources.
> 
> ```
> Login
>   ↓
> Access Token
>   ↓
> API
>   ↓
> Resource
> ```
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> Authorization: Bearer eyJ...
> ```
> 
> Access tokens are typically **short-lived**.
> 
> Why?
> 
> If stolen:
> 
> ```
> Short lifetime
>     ↓
> Smaller attack window
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # 8. Refresh Token ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> When the access token expires:
> 
> ```
> Access Token
>      ↓
> Expired
> ```
> 
> Instead of forcing the user to log in again:
> 
> ```
> Refresh Token
>       ↓
> Authorization Server
>       ↓
> New Access Token
> ```
> 
> Architecture:
> 
> ```
> Client
>  │
>  ├── Access Token ─────→ API
>  │
>  └── Refresh Token ────→ Auth Server
>                               │
>                               ▼
>                          New Access Token
> ```
> 
> ### Typical strategy
> 
> ```
> Access token:
> Short-lived
> 
> Refresh token:
> Longer-lived
> ```
> 
> Refresh tokens need stronger protection.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 9. Session ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> Traditional session-based authentication:
> 
> ```
> Login
>   ↓
> Server creates session
>   ↓
> Session ID
>   ↓
> Browser cookie
> ```
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> Browser
>    │
>    │ Cookie: session_id=abc123
>    ▼
> Server
>    │
>    ▼
> Session Store
> ```
> 
> In a distributed system:
> 
> ```
> API-1
> API-2
> API-3
> ```
> 
> they need access to the same session state.
> 
> You can use:
> 
> ```
> Redis
> ```
> 
> as a shared session store.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Session vs JWT
> 
> | Session | JWT |
> | --- | --- |
> | Server maintains session state | Token carries claims |
> | Usually cookie-based | Often sent as Bearer token |
> | Easy server-side revocation | Revocation requires additional design |
> | Shared store often needed at scale | Can be stateless for access-token verification |
> | Good for traditional web apps | Common for APIs/microservices |
> 
> Important:
> 
> > JWT is not automatically "better" than sessions.
> > 
> 
> Choose based on architecture and requirements.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 10. RBAC ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> RBAC = Role-Based Access Control.
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> Admin
> Manager
> User
> ```
> 
> Permissions:
> 
> ```
> Admin
>  ├── create_user
>  ├── delete_user
>  ├── view_reports
> 
> Manager
>  ├── create_user
>  └── view_reports
> 
> User
>  └── view_profile
> ```
> 
> Architecture:
> 
> ```
> User
>  ↓
> Role
>  ↓
> Permissions
>  ↓
> Resource
> ```
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> GET /admin/users
> ```
> 
> Server checks:
> 
> ```
> Authenticated? ✅
> Role = ADMIN?  ✅
> Permission?    ✅
> ```
> 
> Allow.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 11. API Keys ⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> API keys identify an application/client.
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> X-API-Key: abc123
> ```
> 
> Useful for:
> 
> ```
> Server-to-server APIs
> Developer APIs
> Internal services
> Third-party integrations
> ```
> 
> But API keys usually don't provide the same user identity and delegated authorization model as OAuth/OIDC.
> 
> Don't hardcode them:
> 
> ```
> const API_KEY = "abc123"; // ❌
> ```
> 
> Use:
> 
> ```
> Secrets Manager
> Environment variables
> Secret stores
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # 12. HTTPS / TLS ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> HTTP:
> 
> ```
> Client ──────── HTTP ──────── Server
> ```
> 
> Traffic can potentially be observed or modified.
> 
> HTTPS:
> 
> ```
> Client ═════ TLS ═════ Server
> ```
> 
> TLS provides:
> 
> ```
> Encryption
> Integrity
> Server authentication
> ```
> 
> Example AWS architecture:
> 
> ```
> User
>  ↓
> HTTPS :443
>  ↓
> CloudFront / ALB
>  ↓
> Application
> ```
> 
> AWS ACM can manage certificates for supported AWS services.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 13. CORS ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> CORS = Cross-Origin Resource Sharing.
> 
> Suppose:
> 
> ```
> Frontend:
> https://app.example.com
> 
> API:
> https://api.example.com
> ```
> 
> Browser considers these different origins.
> 
> The API can explicitly allow the frontend:
> 
> ```
> Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com
> ```
> 
> ### Important
> 
> CORS is primarily a **browser security mechanism**.
> 
> It is not an authentication mechanism.
> 
> CORS does not protect your API from:
> 
> ```
> curl
> Postman
> server-to-server requests
> ```
> 
> Your API still needs authentication and authorization.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 14. CSRF ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> CSRF = Cross-Site Request Forgery.
> 
> Imagine:
> 
> ```
> User logged into bank.com
>         ↓
> Browser has authentication cookie
>         ↓
> User visits malicious-site.com
>         ↓
> Malicious site attempts request to bank.com
> ```
> 
> If the browser automatically sends the authentication cookie, a state-changing request could potentially be forged.
> 
> Common protections:
> 
> ```
> SameSite cookies
> CSRF tokens
> Origin/Referer validation
> ```
> 
> ### Important distinction
> 
> CSRF is especially relevant to **cookie-based authentication**.
> 
> Bearer tokens kept outside automatically attached cookies have different CSRF characteristics, although other security concerns still apply.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 15. XSS ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> XSS = Cross-Site Scripting.
> 
> Attacker manages to inject malicious JavaScript into a page.
> 
> Conceptually:
> 
> ```
> <script>
>   maliciousCode()
> </script>
> ```
> 
> Potential impact:
> 
> ```
> Steal sensitive data
> Perform actions as user
> Modify page
> Attack other users
> ```
> 
> Prevention:
> 
> ```
> Output encoding
> Input validation
> Content Security Policy
> Safe templating
> Avoid unsafe HTML injection
> ```
> 
> For React:
> 
> ```
> <div>{userInput}</div>
> ```
> 
> is generally safer than directly injecting arbitrary HTML.
> 
> Be particularly careful with:
> 
> ```
> dangerouslySetInnerHTML
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # 16. Encryption ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> Two major concepts:
> 
> ### Encryption in transit
> 
> ```
> Client
>    ║
>  TLS
>    ║
> Server
> ```
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```
> HTTPS
> ```
> 
> ### Encryption at rest
> 
> ```
> Database
>    ↓
> Encrypted storage
> ```
> 
> Examples:
> 
> ```
> RDS encryption
> S3 encryption
> EBS encryption
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Hashing vs Encryption
> 
> Very common interview question.
> 
> ### Encryption
> 
> Can be decrypted with the appropriate key.
> 
> ```
> Plaintext
>    ↓
> Encryption
>    ↓
> Ciphertext
>    ↓
> Decryption
>    ↓
> Plaintext
> ```
> 
> ### Hashing
> 
> One-way transformation.
> 
> ```
> Password
>    ↓
> Hash
>    ↓
> Stored hash
> ```
> 
> You generally **do not decrypt passwords**.
> 
> Use password-specific password hashing algorithms such as:
> 
> ```
> Argon2
> bcrypt
> scrypt
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # 17. Secrets Management ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
> 
> Never do this:
> 
> ```
> const DB_PASSWORD = "MySecret123";
> ```
> 
> And never commit:
> 
> ```
> .env
> ```
> 
> to Git.
> 
> Instead:
> 
> ```
> Application
>     │
>     ▼
> Secrets Manager
>     │
>     ▼
> Database password
> ```
> 
> For AWS:
> 
> ```
> AWS Secrets Manager
> AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store
> ```
> 
> can be used depending on the requirement.
> 
> ---
