# 🔥 Example: Your E-Commerce Application

Imagine your current Node.js application starts as:

```
Node.js
├── users
├── products
├── orders
├── payments
├── inventory
└── notifications
```

Don't immediately create six microservices.

Start:

```
Node.js Modular Monolith
          │
          ├── Users Module
          ├── Products Module
          ├── Orders Module
          ├── Payments Module
          └── Inventory Module
```

Suppose traffic grows:

```
Orders = 5,000 RPS
Products = 500 RPS
Payments = 200 RPS
```

Now you have a reason to separate:

```
                 API Gateway
                      │
          ┌───────────┼───────────┐
          ▼           ▼           ▼
       Product      Order       Payment
       Service      Service     Service
          │           │           │
         DB          DB          DB
```

Then asynchronous workloads:

```
Order
  ↓
OrderCreated
  ↓
Kafka/SNS/SQS
  ├── Payment
  ├── Inventory
  ├── Email
  └── Analytics
```

That's a **much more defensible microservices architecture** in an interview than simply saying:

> "I use microservices because it is scalable."
> 

