# 15. API Gateway ⭐⭐⭐⭐

API Gateway is the **front door** to your backend services.

```
                    Internet
                       │
                       ▼
                  API Gateway
                       │
          ┌────────────┼────────────┐
          ▼            ▼            ▼
       User API      Order API    Payment API
```

---

# 1. Routing ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Gateway decides where the request goes.

```
/api/users/*
       ↓
User Service

/api/orders/*
       ↓
Order Service

/api/payments/*
       ↓
Payment Service
```

Example:

```
GET /api/orders/123
       ↓
API Gateway
       ↓
Order Service
```

---

# 2. Authentication ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Instead of implementing authentication independently in every service:

```
Client
  ↓
Gateway
  ↓
Authenticate
  ↓
Service
```

The gateway can validate tokens or integrate with an identity provider.

However, services should still enforce authorization for sensitive business operations rather than blindly trusting every gateway request.

---

# 3. Rate Limiting ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Example:

```
User 123
   ↓
API Gateway
   ↓
100 requests/minute
```

If exceeded:

```
429 Too Many Requests
```

This protects downstream services.

---

# 4. SSL/TLS Termination ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Instead of every backend service handling public TLS:

```
Client
  │
 HTTPS
  ▼
Gateway / ALB
  │
 HTTP or internal TLS
  ▼
Service
```

The edge component terminates TLS.

In AWS, ALB/API Gateway + ACM are common combinations.

---

# 5. Request Transformation ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Gateway can sometimes transform requests.

Example:

```
Client request
{
  "userId": 123
}
```

Gateway transforms:

```
{
  "user_id": 123
}
```

before forwarding.

Use carefully; too much business logic in the gateway makes it a new monolith.

---

# 6. Logging ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Gateway can log:

```
Request ID
Path
Status code
Latency
Client information
```

Example:

```
GET /orders/123
Status: 200
Latency: 85ms
Request-ID: abc123
```

This becomes very useful for troubleshooting.

---

# 7. Load Balancing ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Gateway or the load-balancing layer can distribute requests:

```
                  Gateway
                     │
              ┌──────┼──────┐
              ▼      ▼      ▼
            Order1 Order2 Order3
```

This provides scalability and availability.

---

# API Gateway vs Load Balancer

This is a common interview question.

| API Gateway | Load Balancer |
| --- | --- |
| API management | Traffic distribution |
| Routing | Load distribution |
| Authentication | Health checks |
| Rate limiting | Target selection |
| Request transformation | High availability |
| API policies | L4/L7 traffic handling |
| Often API-oriented | Infrastructure-oriented |

They can coexist:

```
User
 ↓
API Gateway
 ↓
ALB
 ↓
ECS Services
```

But don't assume you always need both.

---

# 18. CDN ⭐⭐⭐⭐

CDN = **Content Delivery Network**.

The basic problem:

Suppose your server is in:

```
AWS Mumbai
```

A user in:

```
USA
```

requests:

```
logo.png
app.js
style.css
image.jpg
```

Without CDN:

```
USA User
    │
    │ Internet
    ▼
Mumbai Server
```

With CDN:

```
USA User
    │
    ▼
Nearest Edge Location
    │
    ▼
Cached content
```

---

# 1. CDN

A CDN is a globally distributed network of edge locations that caches and serves content closer to users.

```
                 Origin
                   │
          ┌────────┼────────┐
          ▼        ▼        ▼
       Edge A    Edge B    Edge C
          ▲        ▲        ▲
          │        │        │
        Users    Users    Users
```

---

# 2. Edge Locations ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

An edge location is a location where CDN content can be cached closer to users.

Example:

```
User in India
      ↓
Nearby edge
      ↓
Cached image
```

Instead of every request traveling to the origin.

---

# 3. Static Assets ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

CDNs are excellent for:

```
JavaScript
CSS
Images
Fonts
Videos
Static HTML
Downloads
```

For your React/Next.js applications:

```
/_next/static/*
```

and image/static assets are natural CDN candidates.

---

# 4. Image Delivery ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Suppose your hospital/e-commerce application has thousands of images.

Without CDN:

```
Users
  │
  ▼
Application Server
  │
  ▼
Image Storage
```

This can consume application bandwidth.

Better:

```
Users
  │
  ▼
CloudFront
  │
  ├── Cache HIT → return image
  │
  └── Cache MISS
          │
          ▼
         S3
```

---

# 5. Cache ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

### Cache MISS

```
User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
Not cached
 ↓
S3 / Origin
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
User
```

### Cache HIT

```
User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
Cached object
 ↓
User
```

Cache hit is much faster and reduces origin load.

---

# 6. Cache Invalidation ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Suppose:

```
logo.png
```

is cached.

You upload a new version.

Users may still receive the old cached version.

You can:

```
Invalidate cache
```

Example concept:

```
/logo.png
```

or:

```
/*
```

depending on the deployment strategy.

But invalidating everything unnecessarily can reduce cache efficiency.

### Better approach

Use versioned assets:

```
app.abc123.js
app.def456.js
```

New deployment creates a new filename.

Old cached file can remain safely cached.

